# Raw tables are large and regenerable; the .gz copies are what we commit.
*.tsv
corpus/decoded.txt
corpus/keeper.log
corpus/keeper.pid
corpus/keeper.out
corpus/keeper_state.json

# ⛔ EXCEPTION, s30 2026-08-11. The rule above says "regenerable". THIS ONE IS
# NOT, AND THAT IS ITS ENTIRE POINT: `our_matches.tsv` records WHO CREATED EACH
# MATCH, and the platform has no field that says so — `match list --mine` mixes
# our own challenges with opponents' challenges against us, `triggeredBy` is the
# match TYPE not the actor, and sourceMatchAId/BId are null. Nothing can
# reconstruct a row after the fact. An ignored ledger is a ledger that dies with
# one machine, which is the defect it was built to fix.
!our_matches.tsv

# ⛔ SAME EXCEPTION, SAME REASON, 2026-08-13. `version_trees.tsv` joins a platform
# submission (`ourver=125`) to the tree we built it from (`bots/_v197mapcode`) and
# the name it shipped under ("Loki v8"). NOTHING REGENERATES IT: the platform does
# not tell us which local directory produced a version, and the sixteen seed rows
# had to be reconstructed by hand out of prose scattered across coordination.md,
# HANDOVER, PROGRAMME.md and five preregs, each cited at a file:line. Ignoring it
# would mean the dashboard's version labels exist on one machine only, and the
# next lane would re-derive the same anchors from scratch.
!version_trees.tsv
